'''''Deutzia scabra''''', known as '''Fuzzy Deutzia''' or '''Pride-of-Rochester''',<ref>{{cite web | url = https://www.nynhp.org/invasives/species-tiers-table/ | title = ''New York State Invasive Species Tiers'' | publisher = New York Natural Heritage Program, SUNY College of Environmental Science and Forestry | access-date = 9 Sep 2026}}</ref> is a species of flowering plant in the hydrangea family ([[Hydrangeaceae]]) native to Japan and introduced to mainland east Asia, Europe, and North America.<ref name=fna>{{eFloras|1|200010112|volume=12|''Deutzia scabra''|last1=McGregor|first1=Ronald L.|accessdate=2018-11-04}}</ref><ref name=powo>{{cite web|title=''Deutzia scabra''|url=https://powo.science.kew.org/taxon/urn:lsid:ipni.org:names:790898-1|website=Plants of the World Online|publisher=Royal Botanical Gardens Kew |access-date=2018-11-04}}</ref> In its native Japan, it is found from the [[Kantō region]], westward to the islands of [[Kyushu]] and [[Shikoku]].<ref name=Ohwi>{{cite book |last=Ohwi |first=Jisaburo |date=1965 |title=Flora of Japan |publisher=Smithsonian Institution |pages=512–513}}</ref> Its natural habitat is along forest edges and rocky openings.<ref name=Mikawa>[https://mikawanoyasou.org/data/marubautugi.htm ''Deutzia scabra''] (in Japanese), Flora of Mikawa</ref> It is tolerant of disturbance and can be found growing out of stone walls, along roadsides, and in other waste areas.<ref name=Okayama>[http://had0.big.ous.ac.jp/plantsdic/angiospermae/dicotyledoneae/choripetalae/saxifragaceae/marubautsugi/marubautsugi.htm ''Deutzia scabra''] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201116104242/http://had0.big.ous.ac.jp/plantsdic/angiospermae/dicotyledoneae/choripetalae/saxifragaceae/marubautsugi/marubautsugi.htm |date=2020-11-16 }} (in Japanese), Okayama University Plant Ecology Laboratory</ref><ref name=fna/>

''Deutzia scabra'' is a deciduous shrub growing 1–2 meters in height.<ref name=Mikawa/> One way it differs from other ''Deutzia'' of Japan, is by its somewhat dimorphic leaves: those subtending the inflorescence being sessile and slightly clasping, while leaves lower down the stem are petiolate.<ref name=Ohwi/> It produces panicles of white flowers, blooming from May to July.<ref name=Mikawa/>

The [[cultivar]] 'Candidissima' bears double flowers, and is taller than the species at up to {{convert|4 |m|ft|abbr=on}}. It is a recipient of the [[Royal Horticultural Society]]'s [[Award of Garden Merit]].<ref>{{cite web | url = https://www.rhs.org.uk/Plants/74153/Deutzia-scabra-Candidissima-(d)/Details | title = ''Deutzia scabra'' 'Candidissima' | publisher = RHS | access-date = 5 May 2020}}</ref> The pink flowered double 'Codsall Pink' has also received the award.<ref>{{cite web | url = https://www.rhs.org.uk/Plants/96461/Deutzia-scabra-Codsall-Pink/Details | title = ''Deutzia scabra'' 'Codsall Pink' | publisher = RHS | access-date = 5 May 2020}}</ref>

